{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3978","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md@sha256:3fa98ebc426a9030a63685392dea79c9121d0cdab37df81321d6cf14a3f36612"},"owner_reference":"3978","content":"# Architecture Spec: Primary-Quota 403 Classified Retryable (#3978)\n\n**Scope**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py` (primary edit site),\n`gh_client.py` (one import + one call-site edit), `sync_engine.py`/`server.py`/`backends/github_backend.py`\n(verify-only, no interface change). Extends the existing module design in\n[`backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`](../../../plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md)\n\u00a7 \"Error Handling Pattern\" and \u00a7 \"Module: gh_client.py\" \u2014 this document does not redefine that\nmodule map, it adds one classification signal and one shared delay helper inside it.\n\nEffort matches the item's own estimate: one classification branch, one shared delay-conversion\nhelper, one `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` entry (justified below), no new Pydantic models, no\nnew exception classes, no schema change.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\n`sync_state._classify_github_exception`'s 403 branch treats a primary-quota refusal\n(`RateLimitExceededException`, GitHub's \"API rate limit exceeded\u2026\" message, no `Retry-After`) as\n`NON_RETRYABLE` because it only recognizes `Retry-After` or a \"secondary rate limit\" message\nsubstring. The fix adds a third signal \u2014 primary-limit detection mirroring the codebase's own\nexisting `gh_client._is_rate_limited` precedent \u2014 ahead of the same 403 return statement, and adds\none shared helper that converts `x-ratelimit-reset` (epoch seconds) into a bounded delay when\n`Retry-After` is absent. That helper is called from both the REST classifier's\n`retry_after_seconds` and the GraphQL `GitHubRateLimitedError` construction in `gh_client.py`\n(Acceptance Criterion 9), satisfying the orchestrator's \"fix once where all callers route through\"\nconstraint. `sync_state` gains no new import of `gh_client`; the existing one-way dependency\n(`gh_client` imports `sync_state`) is preserved.\n\nTracing the actual PyGithub call path (Q2, \u00a73) surfaced one additional, in-scope, evidence-backed\ngap: PyGithub's own default retry transport can exhaust its budget and raise\n`requests.exceptions.RetryError` \u2014 an `OSError` subclass that is *not* a `GithubException` \u2014 which\ntoday falls into `classify_sync_error`'s generic `OSError` branch and comes out `NON_RETRYABLE`.\nThat defeats this fix's intent on the one path where PyGithub's own retry-until-reset behavior\ngenuinely fails. \u00a73.3 covers why this is in scope despite not being named in the item text.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design Decisions (Q1\u2013Q6)\n\nEach open question from the feature-context artifact gets a decision here. All six are resolved \u2014\nnone deferred.\n\n### Q1 \u2014 Bound and margin for a reset-derived delay\n\n**Decision**: Convert `x-ratelimit-reset` (epoch seconds) to `(reset - now) + 1.0` seconds (the\n`+1s` margin matches PyGithub's own `GithubRetry` precedent, \u00a73.2), clamped to\n`[0.0, _MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS]` where the ceiling is **3900 seconds** (65 minutes \u2014\nGitHub's documented primary window is 60 minutes; the extra 5 minutes tolerates ordinary clock\nskew without accepting a corrupted timestamp, e.g. a value that is off by orders of magnitude).\nWhen the header is absent, non-numeric, or already in the past, the new helper returns `None` \u2014\nit does **not** fabricate a fallback value. `retry_after_seconds`'s existing contract already means\n\"no hint\" in that case, and `sync_engine._attempt_sync`'s existing\n`max(_compute_backoff_delay(attempt), state.retry_after or 0.0)` already supplies the community-\nstandard \"wait at least a fixed floor\" fallback (30s / 120s schedule) without this change needing\nto invent a second fallback mechanism. No change to `sync_engine.py`'s `MAX_RETRIES` or\n`_BACKOFF_DELAYS` \u2014 the existing `max()` combination already lets a real GitHub-supplied delay\ndominate the fixed schedule, which is exactly what Scenario 2 (feature-context) needs.\n\n**Why not a smaller cap or no cap**: a hard 3600s cap sized exactly to the window would reject a\nvalue at 3599.5s after normal skew; no cap at all reopens Gap 1 (an absurd value sleeping the\nevent loop for years on a corrupted header) that AC2 explicitly rules out.\n\n### Q2 \u2014 What actually reaches the classifier, given PyGithub's in-transport retry\n\n**Decision**: Documented in full in \u00a73; the classifier fix is confirmed necessary and reachable,\nand one additional in-scope change (`RetryError` in `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`, \u00a73.3) is\nadded because tracing showed it directly determines whether this fix is observable on REST call\npaths. The in-transport hang itself (GithubRetry sleeping synchronously inside a single PyGithub\ncall, up to ~65 minutes) is upstream PyGithub behavior, not something `_classify_github_exception`\ncan change, and is **not** part of this fix's scope \u2014 see \u00a73.4 for why it is filed as a follow-up\nrather than addressed here.\n\n### Q3 \u2014 Which signal identifies a primary-limit 403\n\n**Decision**: A new `_is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc, message)` predicate, checked inside the\nexisting 403 branch alongside (not replacing) the current `has_retry_after` /\n`_SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER` check. It recognizes the signal from whichever of three places\nPyGithub happened to preserve it (\u00a73.2 explains why more than one is needed):\n\n1. `isinstance(exc, RateLimitExceededException)` \u2014 true whenever PyGithub's own\n   `Requester.createException` recognized *either* a primary or secondary rate-limit message; safe\n   to treat as retryable unconditionally because `createException` only assigns this type when\n   `isRateLimitError()` was already true.\n2. The casefolded message starts with `\"api rate limit exceeded\"` \u2014 GitHub's documented primary-\n   quota wording, duplicated locally (mirrors the existing `_SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER` /\n   `Requester.isPrimaryRateLimitError` precedent) because `sync_state` must not import `gh_client`.\n3. `x-ratelimit-remaining == \"0\"` on the 403 response \u2014 the fallback for the one case where (1)\n   and (2) are both unavailable: PyGithub's `GithubRetry.increment()` raising a **plain**\n   `GithubException(status, content, headers)` when the response body fails JSON parsing (\u00a73.2's\n   \"malformed body\" branch). Headers still pass through intact in that branch even though the\n   message does not, which is exactly why a header-only fallback is needed and why `_is_rate_limited`\n   in `gh_client.py` already carries the identical fallback for the GraphQL path.\n\nVerified non-regression: `GithubException(403, \"Forbidden\", {})` (AC5's fixture) has no headers,\nso signal 3 evaluates `str(None) == \"0\"` \u2192 `False`; signals 1 and 2 are also false; the branch\nstill returns `NON_RETRYABLE`.\n\n### Q4 \u2014 Does the reset-derived delay also cover 429?\n\n**Decision**: Yes. `retry_after_seconds` already scopes its `Retry-After` lookup to\n`{_HTTP_FORBIDDEN, _HTTP_TOO_MANY_REQUESTS}` (403 and 429) \u2014 the new `x-ratelimit-reset` fallback\nuses the identical status set, so a primary-limit refusal gets the same wait hint whichever status\nGitHub chose to send it on, consistent with GitHub's own docs (\"you will receive a 403 or 429\nresponse\") and with `gh_client._is_rate_limited`'s existing 403/429 (plus 400 for GraphQL) scope.\n\n### Q5 \u2014 Header precedence when both are present\n\n**Decision**: `Retry-After` wins, matching the Change Frame and PyGithub's own `GithubRetry`\n(which only computes the primary-limit reset-based backoff in the branch where `Retry-After` is\n*absent* \u2014 see \u00a73.2). Implementation: the new reset-delay helper is only consulted when\n`parse_retry_after_header` already returned `None`; no new precedence logic is needed beyond that\nexisting `if`/fallback shape.\n\n### Q6 \u2014 Is `gh_client.probe_backend_status`'s any-403-is-rate-limited rule in scope?\n\n**Decision**: Out of scope, per the item's own auto-mode default. `probe_backend_status` (line\n~2047) answers a different question (\"is the backend currently rate-limited for status-reporting\npurposes\") through a deliberately independent, coarser check; aligning it with the classifier's\nmore precise signal is a defensible follow-up but is not named by this item's Files or Acceptance\nCriteria, and touching it would add a fourth call site with its own test surface to a change\nwhose entire premise is \"one classification branch.\" **Recommend filing a separate backlog item**\ntitled along the lines of \"align `probe_backend_status`'s 403 handling with\n`_classify_github_exception`'s rate-limit signal\" once this item lands \u2014 not created here, since\narchitecture design for #3978 does not extend to spawning new backlog items.\n\n---\n\n## 3. PyGithub Call-Path Trace (Q2 detail)\n\nTraced against this repo's installed `PyGithub` (`.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/github/`,\nversion pinned by `uv.lock`) and `urllib3==2.7.0`, `requests==2.33.1` (installed versions).\n\n### 3.1 `make_github_client` uses PyGithub's default retry, not no retry\n\n`github_client.make_github_client()` (line 912) calls `Github(auth=..., base_url=..., timeout=...)`\nwith **no** `retry=` argument. `github.MainClass.Github.default_retry = GithubRetry()` (class\nattribute, `MainClass.py:169`) is the default value of the `retry` parameter\n(`MainClass.py:187`), so every client this factory builds carries a live `GithubRetry()` instance\nwith GitHub's own primary/secondary rate-limit awareness. `Requester.__init__` stores it as\n`self.retry`, and `HTTPSRequestsConnectionClass.__init__` (`Requester.py:182-187`) mounts it as\n`requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter(max_retries=self.retry, ...)` on the session \u2014 confirmed by reading\nthat constructor directly, not inferred.\n\n### 3.2 What `GithubRetry.increment()` does with a 403 (`github/GithubRetry.py`)\n\n`GithubRetry.__init__` adds `403` to `status_forcelist` (already includes 500-599) and adds `GET`,\n`POST` to `allowed_methods` (`GithubRetry.py:84-85`). `urllib3`'s connection pool calls\n`retries.is_retry(method, response.status, has_retry_after)` before ever calling `increment()`\n(confirmed in `urllib3/connectionpool.py:943-950`); for a 403 on GET/POST this is `True`, so\n`GithubRetry.increment()` runs **inside the transport layer, before PyGithub's caller sees\nanything**:\n\n- **`Retry-After` header present** \u2192 logs and falls through to `super().increment(...)` (ordinary\n  urllib3 backoff/retry, no primary-limit-aware delay computed here).\n- **No `Retry-After`, response body is valid JSON with a `message`** \u2192 checks\n  `Requester.isRateLimitError(message)`.\n  - **True** (primary or secondary wording) \u2192 computes `retry.get_backoff_time()` for primary as\n    `(reset_epoch - now) + 1` seconds when `X-RateLimit-Reset` is present and parses as a digit\n    string, else `0.0`; for secondary, `secondary_rate_wait` (default 60s). **No exception is\n    raised.** `urlopen()` sleeps that computed backoff (potentially up to ~an hour, synchronously,\n    inside the caller's single PyGithub call) and retries the request, up to `Retry.total`'s\n    budget (urllib3 default **`total=10`**, unmodified by `GithubRetry.__init__`, confirmed by\n    reading `urllib3/util/retry.py:219-228`).\n  - **False** (body parses but doesn't look like a rate limit \u2014 e.g. plain \"Forbidden\") \u2192\n    `raise Requester.createException(response.status, response.headers, content)`, a **normal\n    `GithubException`** (here `RateLimitExceededException` is *not* constructed, since\n    `createException` itself re-checks `isRateLimitError` and got `False`). This propagates\n    straight out of `urlopen()`/`requests` to the caller, reaching `_classify_github_exception`\n    immediately, no sleep. This is the path AC5's plain-`Forbidden` fixture exercises and must\n    keep rejecting.\n- **No `Retry-After`, body fails JSON parsing / has no `message`** \u2192 the code falls into its own\n  exception-wrapping branch and raises a **plain** `GithubException(response.status, content,\n  response.headers)` directly (`GithubRetry.py:126-129`) \u2014 *not* through `createException*, so it\n  is never typed as `RateLimitExceededException` even when the underlying condition is a genuine\n  primary-limit refusal. Response headers (`x-ratelimit-remaining`, `x-ratelimit-reset`) are still\n  attached to this exception, since they come from `response.headers` unconditionally. This is\n  exactly the case Q3's header-based fallback signal (\u00a72, Q3 item 3) exists for.\n\n### 3.3 When `total` is exhausted: `requests.exceptions.RetryError`, not `GithubException`\n\nIf `GithubRetry`'s repeated sleep-and-retry cycle still returns 403 after all `total=10` attempts\n(possible if the account stays over quota across every retry, or clock skew keeps `X-RateLimit-\nReset` appearing not-yet-reached), `super().increment(...)` \u2014 called from inside `GithubRetry\n.increment()`'s own `try` block \u2014 raises `urllib3.exceptions.MaxRetryError`. That block's own\n`except (MaxRetryError, GithubException): raise` re-raises it unchanged (confirmed,\n`GithubRetry.py:191-192`), so it propagates out of `urlopen()` as `MaxRetryError`.\n`requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter.send()` catches `MaxRetryError` and \u2014 because its `.reason` is a\n`urllib3.exceptions.ResponseError` for a status-forcelist exhaustion \u2014 re-raises it as\n`requests.exceptions.RetryError` (confirmed, `requests/adapters.py:662-668`). `RetryError` extends\n`RequestException(IOError)` (confirmed, `requests/exceptions.py:13,132`), i.e. it **is an\n`OSError` subclass and is *not* a `GithubException`.**\n\n`sync_state.classify_sync_error` checks `isinstance(exc, GithubException)` before its generic\n`isinstance(exc, (OSError, ValueError))` fallback (`sync_state.py:562-576`). A `RetryError` skips\nthe `GithubException` branch entirely (this fix's new signal never runs on it) and lands in the\n`OSError` branch, which returns `NON_RETRYABLE` today \u2014 silently defeating this fix's intent for\nthe one scenario where PyGithub's own transport-level retry-until-reset genuinely failed to\nrecover. This is in scope: **add `requests.exceptions.RetryError` to\n`RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`** (`sync_state.py:49-55`), with a comment citing this trace. It is\na one-line, additive change to a tuple that already exists for exactly this class of\ntransport-transient `OSError` subclass, checked in the same branch order, with no new exception\ntype or consumer surface \u2014 sized the same as the rest of this \"Small\" item.\n\n### 3.4 What stays out of scope\n\nPyGithub's own synchronous sleep-inside-a-single-call behavior (\u00a73.2, up to ~65 minutes blocking\none REST call before any exception or success is visible to the caller) is upstream library\nbehavior, not a `sync_state`/`gh_client` classification defect, and is not named anywhere in this\nitem's Files, Acceptance Criteria, or Non-Goals. Changing it would mean passing a different\n`retry=` policy (or `retry=None`) to `make_github_client`, which changes behavior for *every*\nPyGithub call in the process, not just the rate-limit classification path \u2014 a materially larger\nand differently-scoped change that also intersects #3968's \"stop GitHub requests hanging\" intent\nin the opposite direction (removing PyGithub's own backoff would mean *more* exceptions reach the\nclassifier, not fewer, which is a different problem shape). **Recommend filing a separate backlog\nitem** to evaluate whether `make_github_client` should pass a bounded `retry=GithubRetry(total=\u2026)`\nor `secondary_rate_wait=\u2026` override so no single MCP tool call can block for up to an hour inside\nPyGithub's transport layer \u2014 referencing this document's \u00a73.2 trace as the evidence base.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Component Design\n\n### 4.1 `sync_state.py` (primary edit site)\n\n```python\nfrom github import GithubException, RateLimitExceededException  # add RateLimitExceededException\n\n# New module constants (near existing _SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER, line ~334):\n_PRIMARY_RATE_LIMIT_MESSAGE_PREFIX: Final = \"api rate limit exceeded\"\n\"\"\"Casefolded prefix of GitHub's primary-quota refusal message. Mirrors PyGithub's\nRequester.isPrimaryRateLimitError (.venv/.../github/Requester.py:971-976), duplicated here for\nthe same reason _SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER already is: sync_state must not import gh_client.\"\"\"\n\n_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_HEADER: Final = \"x-ratelimit-reset\"\n_RATE_LIMIT_REMAINING_HEADER: Final = \"x-ratelimit-remaining\"\n_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_SKEW_SECONDS: Final = 1.0\n\"\"\"Matches PyGithub's own GithubRetry margin (github/GithubRetry.py: \"plus 1s as it is not clear\nwhen in that second the reset occurs\").\"\"\"\n_MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS: Final = 3900.0\n\"\"\"Ceiling for a reset-derived delay (~65 min). GitHub's primary window is 60 min; see\narchitect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a72 Q1 for the margin rationale.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc: GithubException, message: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether a 403 GithubException carries GitHub's primary-quota-exhausted signal.\n\n    Args:\n        exc: The 403 GithubException being classified.\n        message: exc's message text, already casefolded by the caller.\n\n    Returns:\n        True when PyGithub typed exc as RateLimitExceededException, the message starts with\n        GitHub's documented primary-limit wording, or the response carries an exhausted\n        x-ratelimit-remaining header (the fallback for a response body GithubRetry could not\n        parse as JSON \u2014 see architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a73.2).\n    \"\"\"\n    ...  # no function body per architecture-boundary convention\n\n\ndef rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(headers: Mapping[str, object] | None) -> float | None:\n    \"\"\"Convert an x-ratelimit-reset header into a bounded, non-negative delay from now.\n\n    Shared by this module's own retry_after_seconds and gh_client._graphql_request's\n    GitHubRateLimitedError construction (Acceptance Criterion 9) -- the one place this\n    epoch-seconds-to-delay conversion lives.\n\n    Args:\n        headers: Response headers, case-insensitively matched like parse_retry_after_header.\n\n    Returns:\n        (reset_epoch - now) + _RATE_LIMIT_RESET_SKEW_SECONDS, clamped to\n        [0.0, _MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS], or None when the header is absent,\n        non-numeric, or the computed delay would be <= 0 (already past). Never fabricates a\n        fallback value -- callers already have their own (see retry_after_seconds's docstring\n        and sync_engine._attempt_sync's max() with the fixed backoff schedule).\n    \"\"\"\n    ...\n```\n\n**`_classify_github_exception`'s 403 branch** (only the `looks_retryable` line changes):\n\n```python\nif status == _HTTP_FORBIDDEN:\n    message = _github_exception_message_text(exc).casefold()\n    has_retry_after = parse_retry_after_header(exc.headers) is not None\n    looks_retryable = (\n        has_retry_after\n        or _SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER in message\n        or _is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc, message)\n    )\n    return SyncErrorKind.RETRYABLE if looks_retryable else SyncErrorKind.NON_RETRYABLE\n```\n\n**`retry_after_seconds`'s GithubException branch** (adds the reset fallback after the existing\n`Retry-After` check, inside the same loop iteration \u2014 no change to the function's loop structure,\ncause-chain walk, or the `GitHubRateLimitedError` branch above it):\n\n```python\nif isinstance(current, GithubException) and current.status in {_HTTP_FORBIDDEN, _HTTP_TOO_MANY_REQUESTS}:\n    hint = parse_retry_after_header(current.headers)\n    if hint is not None:\n        return hint\n    reset_hint = rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(current.headers)\n    if reset_hint is not None:\n        return reset_hint\n```\n\n**`RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`** (line 49-55) gains one entry, per \u00a73.3:\n\n```python\nimport requests.exceptions\n\nRETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS = (\n    asyncio.TimeoutError,\n    requests.exceptions.ConnectionError,\n    requests.exceptions.Timeout,\n    requests.exceptions.ChunkedEncodingError,\n    requests.exceptions.ContentDecodingError,\n    requests.exceptions.RetryError,  # new \u2014 see architect-primary-quota-403-retryable.md \u00a73.3:\n                                       # PyGithub's own GithubRetry transport surfaces an\n                                       # exhausted-retry-budget rate limit this way, not as a\n                                       # GithubException.\n)\n```\n\n**`__all__`** gains `\"rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds\"`.\n\n**Docstring**: `classify_sync_error`'s classification-table docstring (lines ~507-543) must be\nupdated in the same change to add the primary-limit-signal bullet and the `RetryError` bullet \u2014\nper the item's own Files section, this is the only design record of these rules.\n\n### 4.2 `gh_client.py` (one import line, one call site)\n\nImport line (line 76) gains the new symbol:\n\n```python\nfrom .sync_state import (\n    RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS,\n    SyncErrorKind,\n    classify_sync_error,\n    parse_retry_after_header,\n    rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds,\n)\n```\n\n`_graphql_request`'s rate-limited branch (lines 965-967):\n\n```python\nexcept GithubException as exc:\n    if _is_rate_limited(exc):\n        msg = f\"GitHub GraphQL rate limit exceeded: {_github_exception_message(exc)}\"\n        retry_after = parse_retry_after_header(exc.headers)\n        if retry_after is None:\n            retry_after = rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(exc.headers)\n        raise GitHubRateLimitedError(msg, retry_after=retry_after) from exc\n```\n\nNo other line in `gh_client.py` changes. `_is_rate_limited` (already correct per the item's Impact\nRadius) is untouched.\n\n### 4.3 Consumers \u2014 verify only, no interface change\n\n- `sync_engine.py::_attempt_sync`/`_sync_loop` \u2014 consumes `classify_sync_error` and\n  `retry_after_seconds` by their existing signatures; behavior changes only because the two\n  functions now return different values for the same inputs. No code change.\n- `server.py::_retryable`/`_failure_verdict` \u2014 same: consumes `classify_github_failure` and\n  `retry_after_seconds` unchanged.\n- `backends/github_backend.py` (six call sites) \u2014 same: consumes `classify_github_failure`/\n  `classify_sync_error` unchanged.\n- `gh_client.py`'s issue-status-map builder (~line 2291) \u2014 same.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Type System Notes\n\nNo new domain identifiers, enums, or Pydantic models. `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` and\n`_is_primary_rate_limit_signal` are pure functions over already-typed inputs\n(`Mapping[str, object] | None`, `GithubException`, `str`), matching the existing style of\n`parse_retry_after_header`/`retry_after_seconds` in the same module. `Final`-typed module\nconstants for the three new numeric/string literals avoid magic values, matching the file's\nexisting `_HTTP_FORBIDDEN`-style constants.\n\nBoundary note: `headers` dicts arriving from PyGithub are `Mapping[str, object] | None` with\ncase-normalized-but-untrusted string values (confirmed: PyGithub lowercases header names but\ndoes not validate their values) \u2014 `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` must tolerate a missing key,\na non-numeric string, and a numeric string representing a timestamp in the past, returning `None`\nfor all three rather than raising, matching `parse_retry_after_header`'s existing tolerance\npattern (`ValueError` caught, not propagated).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Testing Architecture\n\nNo new test infrastructure \u2014 extends the seven existing suites the item's own Impact Radius\nalready named, using the existing `GithubException`/`RateLimitExceededException` direct-\nconstruction pattern (not live HTTP fixtures \u2014 see \u00a73 for why: `_classify_github_exception` is\nunit-tested by constructing the exception directly, independent of whether PyGithub's own\ntransport would have reached it that way in production).\n\n| Test file | New/changed cases |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/test_retryable_classification.py` | Add a `RateLimitExceededException`(403, no `Retry-After`, primary-limit message) case asserting `retryable: true` at the MCP-response boundary (AC1, AC4). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py` | Add cases for `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds`: future reset (positive bounded delay), reset equal to now (clamped to 0, treated as None per contract \u2014 confirm exact boundary), reset in the past (`None`), missing header (`None`), non-numeric header (`None`), and a corrupted far-future value (clamped to `_MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS`, not passed through raw) (AC2). |\n| `tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` | No new cases; run unmodified to confirm the new primary-limit branch is additive (AC6). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py` | No new cases; `test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable` runs unmodified (AC5). |\n| `tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` | No new cases; runs unmodified (AC7). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py` | Add one case: a primary-limit `RateLimitExceededException` wrapped in `ContentUnavailableError` classifies RETRYABLE via the existing cause-chain walk (no new logic, just coverage). Add one case for `requests.exceptions.RetryError` classifying RETRYABLE via the new `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` entry (\u00a73.3, not named in the item's original Acceptance Criteria but required for the fix to be observably effective \u2014 flag this addition explicitly in the task's completion report so review can confirm it against \u00a73.3's evidence). |\n| `backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py` | Add a case for `GitHubRateLimitedError.retry_after` sourced from `x-ratelimit-reset` when no `Retry-After` is present (AC9). |\n| New integration-style case (per AC3, location per implementer's judgment \u2014 likely `test_startup_sync.py` or a `sync_engine`-focused suite) | A primary-limit 403 raised inside `_attempt_sync` retries with a delay rather than setting `SyncStatus.OFFLINE`. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Non-Goals (confirmed, unchanged from the item's own Change Frame)\n\n- `gh_client._graphql_request`'s rate-limit *detection* (`_is_rate_limited`) \u2014 already correct,\n  not touched, only its delay construction gains the fallback (\u00a74.2).\n- The secondary-rate-limit branch \u2014 untouched, still additive-only per Q3/AC6.\n- `gh_client.probe_backend_status` \u2014 out of scope per Q6; recommend a follow-up item.\n- PyGithub's own in-transport retry/backoff configuration (`retry=` argument to\n  `make_github_client`) \u2014 out of scope per \u00a73.4; recommend a follow-up item citing this\n  document's \u00a73.2 trace.\n- No schema, persisted-state, or MCP tool-signature change; `SyncStatus`, `SyncErrorKind`,\n  `BackendUnavailableError`, `GitHubRateLimitedError` all keep their current shape.\n"}